=== Climate change ===
[[File:Everest is melting. What does it tell us about climate change? - Context News.webm|thumb|The icefall is moving by ~1m per day, a rate that has roughly doubled since 2009.|start=29|end=41]]
The base camp for Everest expeditions based out of Nepal is located by [[Khumbu Glacier]], which is rapidly thinning and destabilizing due to [[climate change]], making it unsafe for climbers. As recommended by the committee formed by Nepal's government to facilitate and monitor mountaineering in the Everest region, Taranath Adhikari—the director general of Nepal's tourism department—said they have plans to move the base camp to a lower altitude. This would mean a longer distance for climbers between the base camp and Camp 1. Although officials had initially stated that the move might happen by 2024,<ref>{{Cite news |date=17 June 2022 |title=Nepal to move Everest base camp from melting glacier |language=en-GB |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/science-environment-61828753 |access-date=17 June 2022 |archive-date=17 July 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230717222537/https://www.bbc.com/news/science-environment-61828753 |url-status=live}}</ref> resistance from the climbing and Sherpa communities resulted in the move being put on hold, in part because there is no viable alternative camp location that would allow climbers to pass through the icefall in the safer early morning hours.<ref>{{cite web|title=Nepal Won't Move the Mount Everest Base Camp for Now, Despite Risks |last=Osborne |first=Margaret |url=https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/nepal-wont-move-the-mount-everest-base-camp-for-now-despite-risks-180982284/ |date=2 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230602191836/https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/nepal-wont-move-the-mount-everest-base-camp-for-now-despite-risks-180982284/ |archive-date=2023-06-02 |url-status=live}}</ref>

